{"parentUuid": null, "isSidechain": false, "userType": "external", "cwd": "/home/dev/shopcart-api", "sessionId": "2f6c0d1e-demo-4b7a-9c3e-rate-limiting", "version": "2.1.0", "gitBranch": "feat/rate-limit", "type": "user", "uuid": "f7378fe0-20f2-4288-b277-c8275a4cb911", "timestamp": "2026-09-14T10:02:11Z", "message": {"role": "user", "content": "Add rate limiting to the public API. Anonymous clients keep hammering /search and it's degrading p99 for paying customers. Keep it simple, we deploy behind one nginx."}}
{"parentUuid": "f7378fe0-20f2-4288-b277-c8275a4cb911", "isSidechain": false, "userType": "external", "cwd": "/home/dev/shopcart-api", "sessionId": "2f6c0d1e-demo-4b7a-9c3e-rate-limiting", "version": "2.1.0", "gitBranch": "feat/rate-limit", "type": "assistant", "uuid": "eaec1073-99a5-49e9-ab49-0254e9a15441", "timestamp": "2026-09-14T10:02:15Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "The simplest correct thing here is per-client limiting at the app layer, keyed by API key when present and by client IP otherwise. Since there's a single nginx in front, I could do it in nginx with limit_req, but the paying/anonymous distinction depends on the API key lookup, which nginx doesn't have. So app-layer it is, and the middleware layer in axum is the natural place.\n\nLet me look at how requests are authenticated today before deciding on the key."}]}}
